<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> What's that Sound?</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Rooster!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: communication: Relay messages from teacher to peers.
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can interpret teacher cues and relay sound information (e.g., the teacher says, 'The cat says meow,' and the student says 'meow' to a peer). Can describe and mimic common animal sounds to peers, using simple words, phrases, or gestures (e.g., says 'meow' and points to a picture of a cat when asked what sound a cat makes). Can assist peers in identifying sounds (e.g., points to a picture of a dog after hearing 'woof').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of the lesson's theme and objectives.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with an animal sound, e.g., "Good morning, everyone! Moo!" and asks students to repeat. The teacher then shows flashcards of familiar animals (cat, dog, rooster, cow) and asks, "What sound does a cat say?" encouraging students to mimic the sound. Focus on previously learned vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to relay a sound and its source. The teacher makes a sound (e.g., "Woof! Woof!") and then points to a dog flashcard, saying, "The dog says 'woof'!" The teacher then says, "Listen, listen! I hear 'woof'!" and points to a student, encouraging them to say, "The dog says 'woof'!" to a peer. The teacher explicitly models the sounds /m/ in 'meow' and /r/ in 'rooster' with clear pronunciation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'meow', is it a dog or a cat?" (Expected: "Cat!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does a pig fly?" (Expected: "No!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the scenario and theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "What's that Sound?":</b> The teacher plays short audio clips of various animal sounds (cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig). For each sound, the teacher asks, "What sound is this?" and encourages students to identify the animal or mimic the sound. New animal names (sheep, horse, fish, pig) are introduced with corresponding flashcards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio player with animal sounds, large colorful flashcards for 'cat', 'dog', 'rooster', 'bird', 'cow', 'duck', 'sheep', 'horse', 'fish', 'pig'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying and describing different animals based on their sounds, using simple sentences and exclamations like 'That's a rooster!' and 'The cat says meow.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Listen! Point to the 'cow' when you hear 'moo'." (Teacher plays cow sound, students point to the cow flashcard).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a loud sound (e.g., cow) or a quiet sound (e.g., bird)?" (Students give a thumbs up for loud, thumbs down for quiet, reinforcing adjectives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What animal says 'quack'?" (Teacher encourages choral response: "Duck!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation skills through guided practice activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Relay messages about animal sounds using WH-questions ('What sound is this?'), Present simple ('The cat says meow.'), Exclamations ('That's a rooster!'), and target vocabulary (cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig, say, bark, meow, quack, woof).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Teacher's Echo":</b> The teacher makes an animal sound and says, "The [animal] says..." Students complete the sentence by mimicking the sound and then relaying the full sentence to their elbow partner. E.g., Teacher: "The cat says...", Students: "Meow!" (to partner). Teacher: "The rooster says...", Students: "Cock-a-doodle-doo!" (to partner).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled – "Sound Relay Race":</b> Students sit in two lines. The teacher whispers an animal sound (e.g., "bark") to the first student in each line. This student then whispers the sound to the next, who whispers it to the next. The last student stands up, says the sound aloud, and identifies the animal (e.g., "Woof! That's a dog!"). The first team to correctly relay the sound and identify the animal wins a point. Emphasize /p/ in pig, /q/ in quack, /r/ in rooster.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "Guess My Sound, Tell Your Friend":</b> Students work in pairs. One student (Student A) secretly chooses an animal flashcard and makes its sound. The other student (Student B) listens, guesses, and then turns to a third student (or the teacher) and relays the information: "He says 'meow'! That's a cat!" or "She says 'quack'! That's a duck!" The third student (or teacher) confirms with "Yes, that's right! That's a duck!"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario, focusing on the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description of Task Format:</b> Simulated "Sound Discovery Walk" in the classroom. Students will practice relaying sounds they 'hear' to a partner. This stage prepares them for the actual project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connecting to Theme: "That's a Rooster!" and Scenario: "What's that Sound?":</b> The teacher creates a "sound station" in the classroom or makes various environmental sounds (e.g., tapping on a table, rustling paper, whistling, stomping feet). Students walk around the classroom in pairs. When they 'hear' a sound made by the teacher or at a station, Student A identifies the sound and tells Student B, "I hear 'tap, tap, tap'!" Student B then turns to another pair (or the teacher) and relays, "My friend says 'tap, tap, tap'! That's a table!" or "They hear footsteps! Someone is walking!" The teacher encourages the use of exclamations and simple descriptions like 'loud' or 'quiet'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion:</b> Students are encouraged to use gestures, pointing, and simple phrases to help their peers understand and relay the sounds. Emphasis is on successfully communicating the observed sound and its potential source, even if the language is not perfectly grammatical. The goal is active participation in the mediation process (interpreting and relaying).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>21st Century Skills Project Focus: Sound Discovery Walk</b> - This activity directly simulates the "Sound Discovery Walk," allowing students to practice the core skill of identifying environmental sounds and describing what they hear to others before going outside. The teacher prompts with questions like, "What do you hear?" "Can you tell your friend?"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of learning outcomes and self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on student participation in relaying sounds and information to peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> In small groups of 3-4, students play "Sound Detective." One student (the 'Sound Maker') makes a common animal sound or a simple classroom sound (e.g., 'meow', 'woof', 'tap, tap' on a desk, 'crinkle' of paper). Another student (the 'Sound Interpreter') listens and then relays what they heard to a third student (the 'Reporter'): "He said 'meow'! That's a cat!" or "She made a 'crinkle'! That's paper!" The Reporter then confirms or asks for clarification. The teacher observes groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear mimicry of sounds and clear articulation of words like 'cat', 'dog', 'meow', 'rooster'. Focus on /m/ and /r/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Identification of animals (cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig) and verbs (says).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity in relaying information:</b> Student effectively communicates the sound and its source to a peer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Students work together to interpret and relay sounds.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students show a thumbs up (easy) or thumbs down (hard) to the questions: "Was it easy to tell your friend what you heard today?" "Did you help your friend understand the sound?"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Consolidation of learning and forward planning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite sound to make or share with a friend today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What sound outside do you want to find and tell your friends about?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students relay messages from me to their peers? The whisper chain was very effective for peer-to-peer mediation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to support their mediation skills further? Perhaps providing sentence frames like "I hear a ____" and "It says ____" more explicitly before the actual "Sound Discovery Walk." Some students struggled with the P, Q, R sounds, so more focused phonemic awareness practice could be integrated.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on animal sounds and mediation within the classroom. The next activity will be the actual "Sound Discovery Walk" where students will apply their mediation skills (identifying and relaying environmental sounds) in a real-world context, building on the practice from today's simulated walk.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>